<h2>The duty, decomposed</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Who owes it</h3><p>All hospitals — government, municipal, charitable and fully private. The section was drafted to end the practice of private facilities turning victims toward government hospitals.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>What is owed</h3><p>First-aid or medical treatment, free of cost. In POCSO practice this is read with the Rules' emergency medical care: stabilisation and treatment rendered promptly by the facility the child first reaches.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The reporting leg</h3><p>The hospital must immediately inform the police of the incident — which also connects the child to the protective machinery: the support person, the Child Welfare Committee and compensation processes.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The sanction</h3><p>Non-treatment is criminalised; the obligation is enforceable, not aspirational.</p></div>
