<h2>What emergency care should include for a child victim</h2>
<p>Good practice — reflected in the POCSO framework and the health ministry's protocols for responding to survivors of sexual violence — treats the first clinical contact as having four components: treatment of injuries and medical stabilisation; prophylaxis appropriate to the case, including prevention of infection and, where relevant, emergency contraception for adolescent victims; psychological first aid, with the examination and history-taking conducted by trained personnel in the presence of a person the child trusts; and careful documentation, since the clinical record made in the first hours often becomes significant evidence. None of this may be conditioned on the registration of an FIR, on payment, or on the family's production of documents.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>For families, in the moment:</strong> go to the nearest functioning hospital — public or private — and ask for emergency care, stating that the case involves a child. Do not wash clothes or clean up before the hospital visit if an offence may need to be proved, however strong the instinct. The hospital will inform the police; the family does not lose control of anything by the hospital doing its statutory duty, and the child's statement remains to be recorded through the child-friendly procedures the law prescribes.</p>
</div>

<h2>When the duty is breached</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Record the refusal.</strong> Names, time, the demand made (payment, FIR copy, transfer "advice") — a contemporaneous note or message creates the accountability record.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Escalate within minutes, not days.</strong> Ask for the medical superintendent or the duty administrator; most refusals dissolve when the statutory provision is named to someone senior.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Move to another facility for care first.</strong> The child's treatment outranks the accountability battle; the record travels with you.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Pursue the sanction afterwards.</strong> A complaint to the police regarding the refusal, to the state health authority regulating the establishment, and — in an ongoing POCSO case — a mention before the Special Court, which can direct compliance and inquiry.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why this provision matters to the criminal case</h2>
<p>Beyond its humanitarian core, Section 397 quietly serves the trial. Prompt treatment produces prompt documentation; prompt documentation anchors the prosecution against the delays and pressures that erode child-victim cases. Conversely, defence counsel scrutinise the first medical record closely, and a gap between incident and first contact — sometimes caused by a hospital's unlawful refusal — becomes a cross-examination theme. A legal system that insists on immediate, unconditional care is also building the most reliable evidence it will ever get.</p>

<div class="check">
  <p>At a glance: free treatment at any hospital, immediately, with no preconditions; hospital informs police; refusal is punishable; forensic examination is separate and consent-based; and the first clinical record should be obtained and preserved by the family's counsel at the earliest stage of the case.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Note:</strong> this explainer is for general information only and is not legal or medical advice. This is a sensitive subject; the identity of victims of sexual offences is protected by law.</p>
</div>
